example.paragraph:
Let $\textbf{x} = \begin{bmatrix}
      x_{1} \\
      x_{2}
      \end{bmatrix}$. Compute $\textbf{x}^{T}\mspace{-4mu} A\textbf{x}$ for the following matrices:

paragraph.math:
$$Q\text{(}\textbf{x}\text{)} = \textbf{x}^{T}\mspace{-4mu} A\textbf{x} = \text{[}x_{1} x_{2} x_{3}\text{]}\begin{bmatrix}
      5 & {- 1\text{/}2} & 0 \\
      {- 1\text{/}2} & 3 & 4 \\
      0 & 4 & 2
      \end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}
      x_{1} \\
      x_{2} \\
      x_{3}
      \end{bmatrix}$$

example.paragraph:
Let $Q(\textbf{x}) = x_{1}^{2} - 8x_{1}x_{2} - 5x_{2}^{2}$. Compute the value of $Q(\textbf{x})$ for $\textbf{x} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 3} \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      2 \\
      {- 2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, and $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      {- 3}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$.

Let $P = \text{[}\begin{matrix}
      \textbf{u}_{1} & \textbf{u}_{2}
      \end{matrix}\text{]} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      {1\text{/}\sqrt{2}} & {- 1\text{/}\sqrt{2}} \\
      {1\text{/}\sqrt{2}} & {1\text{/}\sqrt{2}}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. Then $P$ orthogonally diagonalizes $A$, so the change of variable $\textbf{x} = P\textbf{y}$ produces the quadratic form $\textbf{y}^{T}D\textbf{y} = 3y_{1}^{2} + 7y_{2}^{2}$. The new axes for this change of variable are shown in Figure 3(a).
